+
+## DECISION F041 D3 — a preview is the job's project folder run by the harness's own `runtime serve`, `probe` and `stop` verbs through their JSON envelopes; one record per job, `preview.json`, holds a state machine whose link is written only after a probe passed; and `remedy job preview-start` and `preview-stop` drive it now, before the write door does (2026-09-29)
+
+CONTEXT: T002 asks for commands that start and stop a job's preview, whose effect goes through the runtime supervisor's existing mechanics, with the link shown only after a probe passed and every failure shown honestly. The feature file puts supervisor process semantics and the harness's probe logic out of reach. Measured by the reviewer at `8130599a`: the only code that starts a supervised runtime is `_serve_supervisor` in `apps/cli/commands/runtime_cmd.py`, which exits the process through `fail()` and waits up to 90 seconds for its handshake; `packages/runtimes/dev_server.py` imports `subprocess` at module level; `remedy runtime serve`, `probe` and `stop` each answer one JSON envelope under `--json`, and `serve` on a folder with no runtime answers `runtime_config_error` at exit 2; a job's `JobPlan` carries the project it worked on as `repo_path`, while its staging copy leaves out `node_modules`, `venv` and `.venv`; and the write door's import guard forbids `subprocess` in the door's own imports.
+
+CHOSEN: (1) THE PROJECT a preview runs is the job's `repo_path`, the folder a person would run `remedy runtime serve` in, with its dependencies installed; one runtime per project folder is the harness's own rule and a preview inherits it. (2) THE VERBS are run, never re-implemented: `preview_runner.run_runtime_verb` runs `python -m apps.cli.main runtime <verb> --repo <folder> --json` from the Remedy checkout under a 180-second bound and reads its envelope; a result is ok only when the envelope says ok at exit 0. (3) THE RECORD is `<job dir>/preview.json`, schema `remedy.preview.v1`, holding the state (`stopped`, `starting`, `probing`, `live`, `failed`, `not_applicable`), the pending request, the link and port, a reason and two times. `preview_control` holds it and runs nothing: `request_preview` records a start or a stop, and `run_pending` acts on it with the verb callable it is handed. A start serves, records `probing`, then probes: only a passed probe records `live` with the probe's own link; a failed probe records `started but health check failed: <message>` and stops the runtime again; a serve the harness cannot configure is `not_applicable`; any other serve failure is `failed` with the harness's message. A start while starting, probing or live changes nothing. (4) THE COMMANDS `remedy job preview-start <job>` and `remedy job preview-stop <job>` record and act at once, and exit 1 whenever the preview ends anywhere but where they asked. (5) NEXT: the door's commands, the server-side step that acts on their requests, revalidation of a live preview and the idle stop land together in the next round, under their own DECISION.
+
+ALTERNATIVES: moving `_serve_supervisor` out of the CLI into a callable, rejected because it is the supervisor's lifecycle code the feature file puts out of reach and its refusals are process exits; running the staging copy, rejected because it holds no installed dependencies and may already be released; importing `dev_server` to start the runtime in process, rejected for the same reach and because it would bring `subprocess` under the door once the door records requests.
+
+HOW TO REVERSE: delete `packages/orchestration/preview_control.py`, `packages/orchestration/preview_runner.py`, `apps/cli/commands/job_preview_cmd.py`, their three test files, the two catalog entries, the registration in `apps/cli/commands/__init__.py`, the two rows of `docs/guides/exit-codes.md` and the three allowlist lines, and this paragraph.
